+#### Max Consecutive Losses
+
+`MaxConsecutiveLosses` tracks a streak of consecutive losing trades - not total/cumulative profit, but trades lost one right after another.
+Once `trade_limit` losing trades have closed in a row (within `lookback_period` in minutes, or `lookback_period_candles`), trading is stopped for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`). Any winning (or break-even) trade resets the streak.
+
+By default, this evaluates both a per-pair streak (locking only the affected pair) and a bot-wide streak across all pairs (locking all pairs). Set `only_per_pair` to `true` to only track and lock individual pairs, without ever triggering a bot-wide stop.
+
+Once the cooldown period elapses, the pair (or all pairs, for the bot-wide streak) is automatically unlocked again - no manual intervention is required. As with all protections, an active lock is visible in the same way as other automatic pauses (e.g. via the `/locks` Telegram command or the `Locks` tab/endpoint).
+
+The below example stops trading a pair (and, since `only_per_pair` is not set, the whole bot) for 2 hours after 3 losing trades occurred in a row for that pair, considering the last 24 hours (`24 * 1h candles`) of trades.
+
+``` python
+@property
+def protections(self):
+    return [
+        {
+            "method": "MaxConsecutiveLosses",
+            "lookback_period_candles": 24,
+            "trade_limit": 3,
+            "stop_duration_candles": 2,
+            "only_per_pair": False,
+        }
+    ]
+```
+
+!!! Note
+    `lookback_period` (or `lookback_period_candles`) bounds how far back closed trades are considered. Make sure it's large enough to cover the number of trades needed to form the desired streak, otherwise older losing trades that would extend the streak will not be counted.
+
 #### Cooldown Period
 
 `CooldownPeriod` locks a pair for `stop_duration` in minutes (or in candles when using `stop_duration_candles`, or until the set time when using `unlock_at`) after exiting, avoiding a re-entry for this pair for `stop_duration` minutes.
